<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sardar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sardars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sardar is a Persian-derived title meaning chief or commander, used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> for nobles, military leaders and heads of armed bands. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, sardars were noble chieftains who commanded troops, governed provinces and enjoyed considerable autonomy. The title was also used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> chiefs, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> profile refers to sardars in the period of its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sardar.jpg" alt="Sardar" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ein-oddole.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
